WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.780
As a result of the lies

00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:05.460
and the trickery that
went on for eight years,

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.450
lenders wired in nearly $400 million

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:11.453
of loans to ANI over that period, correct?

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:14.880
That's the number I've been told, yes.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.080
A high profile businesswoman
has been charged

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:18.890
in an alleged fraud scheme.

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:20.358
An established leader

00:00:20.358 --> 00:00:25.358
in San Diego's business
community, Gina Champion-Cain.

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:28.723
Local business leader Gina
Champion-Cain was sentenced

00:00:28.723 --> 00:00:31.720
to 15 years in prison today.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:34.510
Gina Champion-Cain was very well known

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:37.660
amongst people who were in
real estate in San Diego,

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:41.780
leasing and marketing,
developers, property managers,

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:43.490
she always had a huge personality.

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:45.990
She was incredibly
charismatic, very magnetic,

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:49.460
very outgoing, and in a
world that was really kind

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:53.470
of in many ways, a macho boys
club, she could really hang.

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:54.710
She liked sports.

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:56.230
She liked hanging out at the bar.

00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:58.840
She liked to play poker and smoke cigars.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:01.000
She liked going to Las Vegas.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.620
I thought she was very affable,

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:06.810
knowledgeable in the real
estate area no question,

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:09.330
definitely a hard charger, qualified

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:11.650
in the arena she was working in.

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:12.670
Why didn't she stop?

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:13.670
How can you continue

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:15.360
to know that you're tricking people

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.050
and think that you're gonna be able

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:19.370
to give them their money back.

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:21.860
It's like she treated me
like a daughter or a niece.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:23.650
And so it was just so disappointing

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:26.577
to see this woman that I
respected so much do this.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:38.020
Despite its sunny reputation,
San Diego has been home

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:40.350
to more than its share
of financial scandals.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:42.290
In the early 70s, the mayor and much

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:43.900
of the city council were indicted

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:45.980
for fixing taxi industry prices.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:49.710
Also in the 1970s, a local
businessman named Conrad Smith,

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.730
who was known as Mr. San Diego,

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:54.210
presided over what was then
the largest bank failure

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:55.210
in history.

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:58.700
Since at least the 1990s, the
city was underfunding pensions

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:00.310
and using the leftover money

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:04.060
to subsidize projects like a
new baseball park downtown,

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:05.150
the same money was used

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:08.293
to help fund the 1996
Republican National Convention.

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:12.260
Our Republican hearts and minds are

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:16.052
in the hospitable city of San Diego.

00:02:16.052 --> 00:02:17.902
The mismanagement of pensions was actually

00:02:17.902 --> 00:02:21.667
what earned San Diego, the
title of "Enron by the Sea."

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:25.670
This world of rapid downtown
development is the backdrop

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:28.210
where Gina made her real estate career.

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:30.960
San Diego, the most attractive point

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:34.453
for investment and speculation
on the Pacific Coast.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.670
I got here in the mid 80s,

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:44.670
San Diego was still relatively
a small military town.

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:46.070
There were some bigger players,

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:51.070
but actually very easy to be
a big fish in a small pond

00:02:51.472 --> 00:02:52.880
at that time.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:55.690
There weren't a lot of
institutional investors.

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:57.560
Gina knew where to be

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:00.670
to rub elbows with the
people that she needed to.

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:05.140
I met her, I believe sometime
not long after I got here

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:06.575
in the late 80s.

00:03:06.575 --> 00:03:09.110
And she was actually consulting

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:13.610
on some real estate projects
in the Gaslamp Quarter.

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:17.230
The Gaslamp itself was designated

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:19.550
as a national historic district.

00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:21.100
So there was some buzz

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:24.440
and there was still a
lot of undesirable uses

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:29.440
in terms of porn shops, tattoo parlors.

00:03:29.535 --> 00:03:31.446
Our paths never crossed.

00:03:31.446 --> 00:03:33.600
We never did a deal together.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:35.783
We were very friendly socially.

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:38.490
She had started her own company

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:41.350
which was called American
National Investments,

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:42.820
the name of which suggests something

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:44.880
of her ambition, I think.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:46.880
After she started going into hospitality

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:49.610
following the recession,
ANI grew very quickly,

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:52.430
they went from having just
a handful of employees,

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:54.612
really in 2011 and 2012,

00:03:54.612 --> 00:03:58.773
to having hundreds by
let's say 2015, 2016.

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:03.390
She had an office above a nightclub

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:07.470
in downtown San Diego
in what was then a sort

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:10.150
of borderline neighborhood
called the Gaslamp.

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:12.510
She'd really used that as a calling card

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:17.020
for many years thereafter to
kind of suggest her status

00:04:17.020 --> 00:04:18.890
as a major developer in the city.

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:21.575
She started appearing
on all kinds of lists,

00:04:21.575 --> 00:04:24.480
50 People To Watch, 40 Under 40,

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:25.950
I think at some point she even appeared

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:29.149
on the cover of San Diego Pets Magazine.

00:04:29.149 --> 00:04:33.259
In 2003, San Diego Magazine ran a cover

00:04:33.259 --> 00:04:35.885
with a headline "Who Owns Downtown?"

00:04:35.885 --> 00:04:39.790
And on the cover was Gina
Champion-Cain standing

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:43.360
beside a much older man
by the name of Bud Fisher.

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:44.640
My partner, Bud Fisher,

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:47.396
who'd been active in the
real estate business,

00:04:47.396 --> 00:04:52.085
since the mid 70s, he
finally was recognized

00:04:52.085 --> 00:04:54.910
in the San Diego Magazine in that issue,

00:04:54.910 --> 00:04:58.010
saw right next to it was Gina Champion,

00:04:58.010 --> 00:05:03.010
right in her full PR glory
on the Monopoly board

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:04.510
with Bud.

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:07.670
At the time I wasn't
aware of any real estate

00:05:07.670 --> 00:05:09.480
that Gina owned.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:12.609
She had at a maximum, maybe
a couple of small stakes

00:05:12.609 --> 00:05:16.340
in relatively small pieces
of property downtown

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:17.173
at that point.

00:05:17.173 --> 00:05:18.610
For those who were just kind

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:20.760
of casually reading San Diego Magazine,

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:22.850
it very likely seemed to them

00:05:22.850 --> 00:05:27.233
that she really was a major
figure in downtown real estate.

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:32.090
It wasn't the real estate
that really bothered me.

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:35.040
I thought she was in
her lane, so to speak,

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:37.110
it's when she started branching off

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:39.412
into these patio restaurants

00:05:39.412 --> 00:05:44.412
and the patio auxiliary businesses.

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:46.490
Walk into the patio on Gold Finch,

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:48.860
just a few minutes from downtown San Diego

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:50.920
and you'll instantly feel at home.

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:52.990
It's really a reflection
of when you come to my home

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:56.160
to eat, this is the atmosphere
you would experience.

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:58.185
From the people I talked
to and worked with,

00:05:58.185 --> 00:06:00.530
they couldn't put two and two together

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:05.530
to figure out how her restaurant
empire was staying afloat.

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:10.545
So that was always to me, kind of like,

00:06:10.545 --> 00:06:12.700
when am I gonna wake up one day

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:16.590
and the newspaper's
gonna give me the answer

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:18.120
to that question, I would say.

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:22.320
Gina pivoted from corporate
real estate development

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:26.170
to more of a focus on hospitality.

00:06:26.170 --> 00:06:29.130
Simultaneously, she was going

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:31.170
into short term vacation rentals.

00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:34.185
She bought a number of beachfront mansions

00:06:34.185 --> 00:06:38.462
in the Mission Beach
neighborhood of San Diego

00:06:38.462 --> 00:06:42.410
and created sort of a pet
friendly Airbnb model.

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:44.100
I mean, you could have got an operator.

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:46.170
You could have got somebody
to run the business.

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:48.910
I'm curious why you
decided to run the business

00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:52.380
where the busboy's late
and where are the napkins

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:54.060
and all this kind of nonsense?

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:56.820
Well, Jim, you and I have
known each other a long time

00:06:56.820 --> 00:07:00.360
and I have a little problem and
I'm kind of a control freak.

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:03.920
And I don't like just giving up things.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:05.710
She also opened a couple of boutiques,

00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:08.060
at least one of which was
in Mission Beach as well.

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:10.080
Those were called Love Surf.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:12.450
I met her within my
first week of being here.

00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:14.825
I pretty much started at
the boutique right away.

00:07:14.825 --> 00:07:16.890
I was the manager and buyer.

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:20.449
I think she just came in
and met me in the shop.

00:07:20.449 --> 00:07:23.630
And I just thought she was awesome,

00:07:23.630 --> 00:07:26.520
very charismatic, very welcoming.

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:28.310
I just kind of felt right at home.

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:30.969
I always thought that these
investments she was taking were,

00:07:30.969 --> 00:07:34.720
I don't know how somebody
can make this percentage

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:37.850
of a return but I trusted it

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:40.900
because a lot of people
that were very high up

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:43.250
and very well respected trusted her.

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:46.547
Every six months, you were
supposed to get 15% return.

00:07:46.547 --> 00:07:51.540
And so you get a promissory
note once you agree

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:52.800
to doing this.

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:55.320
And it says how much you're investing.

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:57.840
It agrees to give you
back your full return

00:07:57.840 --> 00:07:59.500
if you decide to pull out.

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:01.090
And I had inherited some money

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:04.520
and I'm like, okay, I should
probably take the opportunity

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:05.570
to do this.

00:08:05.570 --> 00:08:07.230
I invested 20,000.

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:09.610
I invested pretty close

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:12.900
to when this whole scheme broke out.

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:15.453
So I never got the chance to roll over.

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:18.490
In general,

00:08:18.490 --> 00:08:22.160
her businesses really
tended to hemorrhage money.

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:25.490
Gina had an assistant, unsurprisingly,

00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:28.950
but apparently at some point
Gina's assistant was able

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:30.740
to hire an assistant of her own.

00:08:30.740 --> 00:08:34.170
And that person, Gina's
assistant's assistant,

00:08:34.170 --> 00:08:36.210
then got two assistants.

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:39.450
So it was sort of unclear to
many at the corporate office

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:42.190
what all these people we're
doing with their time.

00:08:42.190 --> 00:08:44.690
in the process of opening
her first restaurant,

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:45.930
Gina learned a little bit

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:49.520
about the way liquor
licensing works in California.

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:52.450
California liquor licenses
can be quite expensive,

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:55.400
up to hundreds of thousands
of dollars in some cases.

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:57.350
A lot of would-be restaurant owners

00:08:57.350 --> 00:08:59.410
or even current restaurant owners

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:01.910
might have trouble coming
up with that much capital

00:09:01.910 --> 00:09:03.020
on short notice.

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:07.290
It occurred to Gina that there
might be a viable service

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:09.870
in making high interest short term loans

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:13.420
to folks who needed to deposit these funds

00:09:13.420 --> 00:09:15.470
into escrow accounts.

00:09:15.470 --> 00:09:19.520
She approached a friend of
hers named Kim Peterson,

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:21.690
who was a prominent investor

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:23.490
and real estate developer himself

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:26.889
in San Diego and
explained the idea to him.

00:09:26.889 --> 00:09:29.999
And Peterson came on
board first as an investor

00:09:29.999 --> 00:09:31.940
and it appeared to him

00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:35.500
that the loans he was making
functioned as advertised

00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:36.830
and they were paying off.

00:09:36.830 --> 00:09:41.010
Peterson proposed to
take a more active role

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:44.040
in bringing on additional
investors himself.

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:46.420
And from there, the
platform really took off

00:09:46.420 --> 00:09:49.620
as Peterson was able to interest a number

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:52.550
of institutional investors,
banks, and hedge funds,

00:09:52.550 --> 00:09:54.510
and so forth to really increase the amount

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:57.880
of capital going into Gina's platform.

00:09:57.880 --> 00:09:59.820
A very substantial amount of capital came

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:03.010
from institutional investors,
notably a hedge fund based

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:07.593
in Austin called Ovation as
well as Bank of California.

00:10:13.370 --> 00:10:16.490
The problem with this
premise is that in fact,

00:10:16.490 --> 00:10:18.950
the Liquor Licensing
Board only requires them

00:10:18.950 --> 00:10:21.930
to deposit five or 10% let's say,

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:24.422
and they only have to come
up with the full amount

00:10:24.422 --> 00:10:27.670
when they actually
acquire the full license

00:10:27.670 --> 00:10:30.160
or the restaurant that the
license is coming with.

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:35.020
There is no strong demand for
short term high interest loans

00:10:35.020 --> 00:10:37.670
to cover these escrow costs.

00:10:37.670 --> 00:10:38.640
As far as we can tell,

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:40.970
there were no loans ever actually made

00:10:40.970 --> 00:10:42.020
through this platform.

00:10:42.020 --> 00:10:45.330
A little bit under $400 million
are thought to have flowed

00:10:45.330 --> 00:10:46.680
through the account.

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:49.790
Early investors certainly
were making money.

00:10:49.790 --> 00:10:51.990
I don't think it was
about not going to prison.

00:10:51.990 --> 00:10:54.450
I think it was to keep making people a lot

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:56.210
of money, which I was doing.

00:10:56.210 --> 00:11:01.210
In early 2019, there was a
very big private equity fund

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:06.820
and real estate developer
that was considering investing

00:11:06.820 --> 00:11:08.710
about $100 million in the fund.

00:11:08.710 --> 00:11:10.260
But before they pulled the trigger,

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:12.080
they wanted an expert read.

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:16.040
And so they hired a guy
named Michael Brewer,

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:19.180
who is one of the foremost experts

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:21.450
on California liquor
licensing in the state

00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:24.420
to design sort of a due
diligence process for them.

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:26.460
And in the course of that due diligence,

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:28.820
Brewer and his client concluded

00:11:28.820 --> 00:11:31.870
that Gina's loan program
wasn't actually funding

00:11:31.870 --> 00:11:33.620
any liquor license escrows

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:35.390
and that the most likely explanation

00:11:35.390 --> 00:11:37.740
for its success was that
it was a Ponzi Scheme.

00:11:41.150 --> 00:11:43.510
All of a sudden we just got an email

00:11:43.510 --> 00:11:44.600
from our HR department.

00:11:44.600 --> 00:11:48.670
I don't remember the exact verbiage

00:11:48.670 --> 00:11:52.870
but it was something like
this story has been released.

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:55.210
The SEC is investigating this,

00:11:55.210 --> 00:11:58.650
just please don't talk to any press.

00:11:58.650 --> 00:12:00.380
The Securities and Exchange Commission

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:02.170
says the company defrauded dozens

00:12:02.170 --> 00:12:05.660
of retail investors out of
hundreds of millions of dollars.

00:12:05.660 --> 00:12:08.610
I completely thought I
was a misunderstanding.

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:11.340
I was like, there's no way
that this could be happening.

00:12:11.340 --> 00:12:13.320
I thought I was gonna have my job forever.

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:15.060
I loved that company.

00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:17.023
And it's kind of like the denial phase.

00:12:17.023 --> 00:12:19.940
You think that it can't be true.

00:12:19.940 --> 00:12:23.390
Somebody I love and I
respect, they can't do this

00:12:23.390 --> 00:12:26.610
to people that they are supposed to love.

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:28.460
Part of the success of the fund I think,

00:12:28.460 --> 00:12:30.020
was Gina's very good standing

00:12:30.020 --> 00:12:32.084
in the business community in San Diego.

00:12:32.084 --> 00:12:35.040
Part of it was also Kim
Peterson's very good standing,

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:37.400
people really respected Kim Peterson.

00:12:37.400 --> 00:12:39.050
They thought of him as an honest guy

00:12:39.050 --> 00:12:41.223
and as a very careful investor.

00:12:54.800 --> 00:12:56.490
Gina had very close relationships

00:12:56.490 --> 00:12:59.360
with a handful of
employees at Chicago Title,

00:12:59.360 --> 00:13:02.272
which was the company
overseeing the escrow accounts

00:13:02.272 --> 00:13:03.105
for her.

00:13:03.105 --> 00:13:04.780
And she maintained those relationships

00:13:04.780 --> 00:13:07.647
in part by giving very nice gifts.

00:13:07.647 --> 00:13:11.370
Free baseball tickets,
Chargers suite tickets,

00:13:11.370 --> 00:13:15.010
when the Chargers were still in San Diego,

00:13:15.010 --> 00:13:18.650
Petco Park, we had corporate suites there.

00:13:18.650 --> 00:13:22.544
I gave lots of bottles
of alcohol, champagne,

00:13:22.544 --> 00:13:27.544
jewelry, cash, lots of free
food at my restaurants.

00:13:29.620 --> 00:13:31.958
In a message that was cited
in a number of lawsuits,

00:13:31.958 --> 00:13:35.047
Gina said to two Chicago employees,

00:13:35.047 --> 00:13:38.340
"If anyone calls asking
about escrow accounts

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:40.820
and alcohol licenses, blah, blah, blah,

00:13:40.820 --> 00:13:43.630
just tell them show me
the money, ha ha ha ha."

00:13:43.630 --> 00:13:45.040
We should emphasize actually,

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:49.760
that many investors put their
trust more in Chicago Title,

00:13:49.760 --> 00:13:51.540
at least that's what they would say now,

00:13:51.540 --> 00:13:53.800
than they did in Gina Champion-Cain.

00:13:53.800 --> 00:13:54.860
Chicago title is one

00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:57.360
of the largest title insurance
companies in the country.

00:13:57.360 --> 00:13:59.510
Chicago Title as the
keeper of the escrows,

00:13:59.510 --> 00:14:02.173
was not supposed to allow money

00:14:02.173 --> 00:14:03.810
in or out of any account

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:07.590
for any purpose other than
the account holder specified.

00:14:07.590 --> 00:14:08.423
In retrospect,

00:14:08.423 --> 00:14:12.373
Gina's Ponzi Scheme might have
been quite easy to uncover.

00:14:12.373 --> 00:14:15.310
All it might have taken was a phone call

00:14:15.310 --> 00:14:18.011
to one or two people who were supposedly

00:14:18.011 --> 00:14:21.280
financing their liquor
license escrow accounts

00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:24.593
with her fund to find out
that in fact they were not.

00:14:25.530 --> 00:14:28.480
When we think of the largest
fraudsters in history,

00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:30.576
it's mens' names that come to mind,

00:14:30.576 --> 00:14:34.170
most notably Bernie
Madoff, but Gina is thought

00:14:34.170 --> 00:14:37.853
to be the most prolific female
Ponzi Schemer in history.

00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:39.900
a very substantial amount

00:14:39.900 --> 00:14:42.400
of capital came from
institutional investors

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:44.980
but there were a lot of people
that Gina knew personally

00:14:44.980 --> 00:14:46.433
who put in money as well.

00:14:48.330 --> 00:14:50.613
She reached out to me through text message

00:14:50.613 --> 00:14:53.480
and asked if I wanted to come over.

00:14:53.480 --> 00:14:55.840
She said she wanted to
explain the whole situation.

00:14:55.840 --> 00:14:57.660
And so I was like, okay, I'm
gonna give her the benefit

00:14:57.660 --> 00:14:59.010
of the doubt because it's like, okay,

00:14:59.010 --> 00:15:00.880
I still don't have all the facts.

00:15:00.880 --> 00:15:02.820
And then she sat me down.

00:15:02.820 --> 00:15:07.430
We shared some wine and
she told me the story

00:15:07.430 --> 00:15:10.620
from when she started
until she got caught.

00:15:10.620 --> 00:15:13.099
And she said that it
started off legitimately.

00:15:13.099 --> 00:15:15.870
And then she just liked this feeling

00:15:15.870 --> 00:15:19.288
of getting money and
being able to hire people

00:15:19.288 --> 00:15:20.950
and building this empire.

00:15:20.950 --> 00:15:22.663
And she said she got too carried away

00:15:22.663 --> 00:15:25.099
and it slipped way out of her hands.

00:15:25.099 --> 00:15:27.826
She was promising me all my money back.

00:15:27.826 --> 00:15:30.860
She was promising me
that I was gonna be part

00:15:30.860 --> 00:15:32.649
of a Netflix documentary.

00:15:32.649 --> 00:15:34.897
And still I was like,

00:15:34.897 --> 00:15:37.710
how is she gonna have a Netflix deal?

00:15:37.710 --> 00:15:39.080
She's gonna be in prison.

00:15:39.080 --> 00:15:41.310
Gina lived a very nice lifestyle.

00:15:41.310 --> 00:15:43.850
She had property in Carmel, California.

00:15:43.850 --> 00:15:48.339
She had property out in Palm Desert.

00:15:48.339 --> 00:15:51.480
She had a very nice home in Mission Hills,

00:15:51.480 --> 00:15:54.500
but most of the money
does seem to have gone

00:15:54.500 --> 00:15:55.920
to the businesses.

00:15:55.920 --> 00:15:57.500
Well, I truly believed I was

00:15:57.500 --> 00:16:00.023
gonna take a couple these companies public

00:16:00.023 --> 00:16:01.580
and pay everybody back.

00:16:01.580 --> 00:16:04.363
Not that what I was doing
was right, 'cause it wasn't.

00:16:05.270 --> 00:16:07.434
Her businesses went into receivership

00:16:07.434 --> 00:16:12.434
and have been gradually
getting liquidated,

00:16:13.160 --> 00:16:17.273
sold off, chopped up, and sold for pieces.

00:16:17.273 --> 00:16:20.330
I still can't believe she
pulled it off, I guess,

00:16:20.330 --> 00:16:24.200
as long as she could,
but it is disappointing.

00:16:24.200 --> 00:16:27.210
Sorry, it is disappointing to see somebody

00:16:27.210 --> 00:16:31.790
that you loved and really
looked up to do this.

00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:36.140
And I don't wanna associate other people

00:16:36.140 --> 00:16:39.748
with being liars and deceiving me.

00:16:39.748 --> 00:16:42.940
And it was really hard
for me to trust anybody

00:16:42.940 --> 00:16:44.700
when I was applying for jobs again

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:47.240
because every company
I looked at I'm like,

00:16:47.240 --> 00:16:48.410
well how do you get your money?

00:16:48.410 --> 00:16:50.210
How do you operate this business?

00:16:50.210 --> 00:16:52.440
We know that the Department of Justice is

00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:54.650
actively investigating a number

00:16:54.650 --> 00:16:56.860
of former Chicago Title employees.

00:16:56.860 --> 00:16:59.350
There are also a number
of ongoing civil suits

00:16:59.350 --> 00:17:00.583
against Chicago Title.

00:17:18.270 --> 00:17:21.490
She pled guilty and was
ultimately sentenced to 15 years

00:17:21.490 --> 00:17:22.960
in Federal prison.

00:17:22.960 --> 00:17:26.160
She is currently in a
minimum security institution

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:28.463
in Northern California called FCI Dublin.

00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:37.366
I am living my life as a
very good prisoner here

00:17:37.366 --> 00:17:39.180
at Dublin.

00:17:39.180 --> 00:17:42.020
I teach classes, I mentor people,

00:17:42.020 --> 00:17:45.443
and I'm a very strong member
of this community here.

